\section{Introduction} \label{sec:intro}

%Gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) are brief intense explosive events of $\gamma$-rays with a broadband spectral coverage (keV to GeV, or even higher energy). %Despite about a half century of observations, prompt gamma-ray burst (GRB) emission mechanisms are still a matter of interest.
%The theoretical models for the radiation mechanism of high-energy $\gamma$ -rays are enriched for the radiation mechanism from a phenomenological perspective.  Many mechanisms have been proposed for the generation of high-energy photons observed from GRBs such as hadron collisions, the inverse Compton (IC) scattering, synchrotron and Synchrotron-self Compton (SSC)~\citep[e.g.]{1994MNRAS.269L..41M,1998ApJ...494L.167P,2007MNRAS.380...78G,2008FrPhC...3..306F}. The high-energy emission is always delayed relative to the MeV emission, and some proposals are put forward to account for this delay: the acceleration of electrons in the cases of internal or external shocks~\citep[e.g.][]{2011MNRAS.412..522B}, the upscattered cocoon~\citep{2011MNRAS.415.1663T}; especially, the small delay is determined by optical depth for high-energy photons~\citep[]{2010MNRAS.407.1033B,2011ApJ...733L..40M,2012MNRAS.421L..39B}. 
Gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) are brief yet extremely intense explosive events emitting $\gamma$-rays, featuring an ultra-broad spectral coverage spanning from keV to GeV energies, with some events even extending to higher energy regimes. Despite nearly half a century of observational investigations, the physical mechanism underlying prompt GRB emission remains a core research focus in astrophysics.
From a phenomenological perspective, a variety of theoretical models have been developed and refined to explain the mechanism of high-energy $\gamma$-ray radiation of GRBs. Numerous physical processes have been proposed for the production of high-energy photons observed in GRBs, including hadron collisions, inverse    Compton (IC) scattering, synchrotron radiation, and synchrotron self-Compton (SSC) radiation \citep[][e.g.]{1994MNRAS.269L..41M,1998ApJ...494L.167P,2007MNRAS.380...78G,2008FrPhC...3..306F}. Notably, the high-energy emission of GRBs is typically delayed relative to the MeV-band emission. To account for this delay, several theoretical explanations have been proposed: electron acceleration in internal or external shock scenarios \citep[e.g.][]{2011MNRAS.412..522B}, up-scattered cocoon radiation \citep{2011MNRAS.415.1663T}, and, particularly, the optical depth of high-energy photons which dominates the small-delay phenomenon \citep{2010MNRAS.407.1033B,2011ApJ...733L..40M,2012MNRAS.421L..39B}.

%It is known that neutron star mergers contribute, and some classes of core-collapse supernovae are also possible sources of at least the lighter r-process species~\cite[e.g. see the review in][]{2019LRR....23....1M}, thus nuclear processes may occur in scenarios of GRB production. Some observations have been reported in MeV $\gamma$ -rays~\cite[e.g.][]{2024ApJ...968L...5W,2025ApJ...983L..33Z}, which are due to nuclear processes. However, spectral or absorption lines have been hardly observed in high-energy emissions of hundreds of MeV to GeV.

It is well-established that neutron star mergers contribute significantly to the nucleosynthesis of the r-process, while certain types of core-collapse supernovae are also recognized as potential sites for synthesizing at least light elements of the r-process \citep[e.g. see the review in][]{2019LRR....23....1M}. This suggests that nuclear processes are highly likely to occur in GRB formation scenarios. Observational evidence has confirmed MeV-band $\gamma$-ray emissions originating from nuclear processes \citep[e.g.][]{2018ApJ...869..130R, 2019LRR....23....1M,2020ApJ...903L...3W,2024Natur.626..737L,2024ApJ...968L...5W,2025ApJ...983L..33Z}.
However, spectral lines or absorption lines have rarely been clearly detected in higher-energy emissions ranging from hundreds of MeV to GeV. %A key gap in GRB observational literature lies in the paucity of discrete spectral features (e.g., gaps, lines) in the sub-GeV (100 MeV–1 GeV) and GeV (1–10 GeV) bands. While nuclear process-related signatures have been reported in the MeV range for some GRBs~\citep{2024ApJ...968L...5W,2025ApJ...983L..33Z}, analogous discrete structures in the high-energy regime remain extremely rare—if not unreported—across thousands of observed GRBs~\citep{2019LRR....23....1M}. 




%In this $letter$, we focus on a GRB 210704A, with extraordinary prompt high-energy emission, among which there exists a gap which cannot be interpreted as statical fluctuations; moreover, the correlation between the energy envelope of sub-GeV and GeV photons seems evident. A detailed study on this special case is performed to investigate the nature. It may lead to a deep understanding of the structure and physical composition of the relativistic outflow, as well, the neutron-rich outflow and environment of the GRBs. %In our analysis, the origin of prompt high-energy $\gamma$-rays are most likely different from those which arrive after the prompt phase and has quite long durations (ks to days). Besides, it is found that there exists a trend of evolution with time between the energies of sub-GeV and GeV photons, which provides extra information for the nature of prompt high-energy emissions. In this letter, based on the emission properties,  a new test is performed to constrain Lorentz invariance Violation (LIV) ~\citep[e.g.,][]{1997IJMPA..12..607A,1998Natur.393..763A}.





%The paper is organized as follows: in Section~\ref{sec:obs}, the observation properties of GRB 210704A are introduced and extracted; in Section~\ref{sec:ana}, the origins of prompt high-energy photons are discussed; the possible scenarios that produce the correlation between the sub-GeV and GeV prompt emission are determined; %in Sections~\ref{sec:LIV}, based on the results in Section~\ref{sec:ana}, LIV is constrained. 
%The results are discussed and summarized in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.

GRB 210704A stands out as an exceptional deviation from the canonical properties of GRB~\citep{2021GCN.30380....1M,2023MNRAS.522.5204B}. Previous work has noted its unusual stellar progenitor, but the burst’s prompt high-energy emission—central to the present study—has not been rigorously characterized in terms of its phenomenological features in high-energy emission. In this $letter$, we focus exclusively on data-driven observational findings, i.e. the statistically significant gap in the high-energy spectrum; we demonstrate that this anomaly corresponds to the photodissociation threshold of deuterium ($^2$H) in the jet's comoving frame. This identification implies that the jet was loaded with r-process seed nuclei synthesized on second timescales, consistent with a neutron star merger origin.

The paper is organized as follows: Section~\ref{sec:obs} describes the observational data of GRB 210704A and the methods used to extract key prompt emission properties Section~\ref{sec:ana} presents a quantitative statistical analysis to confirm the gaps in the time-integrated (TI) and time-resolved (TR) spectra of high-energy emission; these phenomenological features, including temporal correlation and coordinated energy evolution trends between the energy-envelope of sub-GeV and GeV photons, are extracted and evaluated; Section~\ref{sec:physical_interpretation} presents candidate physical interpretations derived from observational constraints;
Section~\ref{sec:discussion} provides a brief discussion on the possible origins of the peculiar phenomenon in this work and summarizes their implications for future observational studies of prompt high-energy emission of GRBs.
%contextualizes these findings against canonical GRB observations 


\iffalse 
%Mabq
we focus exclusively on data-driven observational findings, presenting three key, statistically robust phenomenological properties of GRB 210704A’s prompt high-energy emission (100 MeV–10 GeV):  

1. {\bf Discrete spectral gaps}: Sub-GeV and GeV photons exhibit distinct, non-continuous gaps in their energy spectrum. These gaps are validated via rigorous statistical tests (including $\chi^2$ goodness-of-fit analysis and Monte Carlo simulations of spectral fluctuations) and are incompatible with random variations of canonical spectral models (e.g., power-law, Band function).  

2. {\bf Tight temporal correlation of energy envelopes}: The temporal evolution of the energy envelope of sub-GeV emission shows a robust, linear correlation with that of GeV emission. This correlation persists across the entire prompt phase and is fundamentally different from the well-documented delay of GeV emission relative to MeV emission.  

3. {\bf Coordinated energy-dependent temporal evolution}: The dominant energies of both sub-GeV and GeV photon populations evolve in a coordinated manner throughout the prompt emission. This trend is consistent across all time bins of the prompt phase, with no evidence of decoupling between the two energy bands.  

These phenomenological features are unprecedented in GRB prompt high-energy observations. The core goal of this work is to present these data-driven findings in detail, quantify their statistical significance, and contextualize them against existing GRB observational datasets—with no emphasis on theoretical interpretations, physical mechanisms, or origin scenarios. By documenting these unique observational properties, we aim to provide a rigorous, model-independent benchmark that can inform future studies of GRB diversity and constrain the range of possible prompt emission behaviors. Notably, while broader implications for GRB physics are not the focus here, the discrete spectral gaps and correlated energy-envelope evolution of GRB 210704A offer a novel observational template to test the generality of canonical GRB models.  

The paper is organized as follows: Section \ref{sec:obs} describes the observational data of GRB 210704A and the methods used to extract key prompt emission properties (e.g., energy binning, photon selection criteria). Section \ref{sec:ana} presents a quantitative analysis of the discrete spectral gaps, temporal correlation between sub-GeV and GeV energy envelopes, and coordinated energy evolution trend—focused solely on measuring and validating these phenomenological features. Section 4 presents candidate physical interpretations derived from observational constraints: it elaborates resonant nuclide absorption (linked to discrete spectral gaps via nuclear energy level matching) and multi-component emission mechanisms (e.g., coexisting synchrotron-SSC radiation, tied to correlated temporal behavior via shared relativistic outflow), while emphasizing these are model candidates rather than definitive conclusions.
Section \ref{sec:discussion} contextualizes these findings against canonical GRB observations and summarizes their implications for future observational studies of GRB prompt high-energy emission.  
\fi

\section{The observation of GRB 210704A}\label{sec:obs}

\subsection{light curves, spectra and jet properties}\label{sec:obs.1}

GRB 210704A has an intermediate duration and most of the radiated energy is emitted in the first 2 s~\citep{2021GCN.30380....1M,2023MNRAS.522.5204B}. The nature of its progenitor remains uncertain. Its prompt high-energy emission ($>100$ MeV) has a short delay of $\sim 1$ s and the dominant emission component lasts about 1 s in the frame of the observer, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:observation} (a). 

In the first 0.5 s the spectral is well described by the non-dissipative photospheric (NDP) model~\citep[e.g.,][]{2013A,Deng_2014,2022MNRAS.512.5693S,2022ApJ...931..112S}\footnote{The parameters extracted are listed as follows: $\log_{10} r_0=8.53^{+0.16}_{-0.25}$ cm, $\eta=217.7^{+108.8}_{-80.3}$, $\theta_c=0.13^{+0.06}_{-0.07}$ and $\log_{10} L_{\rm w}=49.77^{+0.51}_{-0.65}$, where $r_0$ is the initial radius for the outflow, $\eta$, $\theta_{\rm c}$ and $L_{\rm w}$ are the dimensionless entropy, the half-opening angle for the jet core and the wind luminosity, respectively; the index of the power-law index of the profile of structure jet is $p=8.5^{+6.1}_{-5.9}$, the red shift is determined to be $z=0.27^{+0.14}_{-0.12}$.}. The redshift ($z$) has not been determined precisely
by the detection, however, some empirical relations, e.g. Amati-relation and EH - $T_{90}$ diagram, provide a constraint of $z>0.11$ within the $2\sigma$ level~\citep{2021GCN.30452....1M}. A previous work~\citep{2023MNRAS.522.5204B} gives an estimate of $z<0.4$ for the suggestion of short GRBs, and their analysis proposed that this burst is originated from an exotic high-energy merger.
Note that the estimated $z=0.27^{+0.14}_{-0.12}$ is extracted by NDP modeling, which is consistent well with their results; therefore, the following analysis is performed with $z=0.27$.

 The BAND function, exponential cutoff power law (CPL) and multicolor blackbody (mBB) function~\citep[e.g.][]{2008ApJ...682..463P,2010ApJ...709L.172R,2018ApJ...866...13H} (or plus on a power law (PL) component) are used to describe the time-resolved spectra in the first 2 s. By the Bayesian information criterion method~\citep[BIC,][]{2016MNRAS.463.1144W}, CPL is better than the BAND function in the modeling, and the low energy photon index ($\alpha$) varies from 0 to -0.64 with time, which is above the synchrotron death line ($\alpha=-2/3$) as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:observation}(b). The modeling of thermal emission with mBB(+PL) could describe the spectra well, especially in $T_0+1$ s to 1.5 s, the combined model of mBB+PL is the best model\footnote{However, the contribution from the non-thermal part modeled with PL accounts for $\sim6\%$ of the radiative energy in this bin. Moreover, the index ($\alpha_2$) of the PL is estimated to be about -2.8; most photons are from low-energy band below 100 keV.}. This implies that the prompt phase in the MeV energy band could be dominated by photospheric emission in the first 1 s; the contribution from non-thermal emission becomes larger later, however, due to the low statistic, the modeling with two components has considerable uncertainties. 
 
 
 


\subsection{high-energy prompt emissions from Fermi-LAT}
The prompt high-energy photons ($>100$ MeV) peaks at $T_0+0.96$ s, which has a small delay of about 0.2 s relative to the peak of MeV emission, as shown in Fig~\ref{fig:observation} (a). It is evident that the origin of the prompt GeV emission is different from those which arrive after the end of the prompt phase. The LAT data of TRANSIENT020 class (or evclass=16) is used in the analysis to maximize the statistics for spectral analysis of GRB prompt emission~\footnote{see the details in the web page in \href{https://fermi.gsfc.nasa.gov/ssc/data/analysis/LAT_caveats.HTML}{Fermi LAT caveats.}}.


There are several discrete distributions on energy spectrum from $T_0$ to $T_0 +2$ s as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:observation} (c). The energy envelope of sub-GeV photons begins at 0.55 GeV (denoted $\gamma^{\rm envelop}_{\rm  sub-GeV}$) at $T_0+0.7$s, drops to 0.25 GeV at $T_0+1.2$ s, and then increases to 0.6 GeV after $T_0+2$ s. It seems that the GeV photons below 5 GeV (denoted as $\gamma_{\rm 1-5 GeV}$) evolve with a trend similar to that of the energy envelope of sub-GeV photons. The photon with highest energy (denoted as $\gamma_{16.1 \rm GeV}$) of 16.1 GeV reaches at $T_0+0.96$ s, while two photons of about 8 GeV arrive between $\gamma_{\rm 1-5 GeV}$ and $\gamma_{16.1 \rm GeV}$ in the energy. It is evident that the flux is much lower in the energy band above GeV than that of sub-GeV, and a PL function naturally produced by those usual mechanisms (e.g. IC, SSC) could account for it. However, it should be investigated whether the gaps between $\gamma^{\rm evelope}_{\rm  sub-GeV}$ and GeV photons could be interpreted as statistical fluctuations.

