\subsection{The possible correlation between  $\gamma^{\rm envelop}_{\rm sub-GeV}$ and $\gamma_{\rm 1-5GeV}$}\label{sec: correlation}

The Spearman rank correlation coefficient ($\rho$) is used to quantify the monotonic relationship of the temporal evolution between the energy of GeV photons and that of $\gamma^{\rm envelop}_{\rm sub-GeV}$. This nonparametric approach provides a robust correlation measure that is insensitive to outliers and does not rely on linear assumptions~\citep{spearman}\footnote{The spearman rank correlation coefficient $\rho$ is defined as $\rho = \frac{\mathrm{cov}(r(x),r(y))}{\sigma_x \sigma_y} $ where $r(a)$ represents the rank of element $a$. For non-duplicated datasets, $\rho$ can be interpreted as $\rho = 1 - \frac{6 \Sigma d^2_i}{n(n^2-1)} $ where $d_i = r(x) - r(y)$.}.
In order to ensure a consistent one-to-one correspondence between data points, the energy envelope of sub-GeV photons was extracted as follows: each time point of GeV photons is defined in a bin, and the maximum sub-GeV energy value in that bin was adopted. To reduce the impact of human choices, different bin edges are traversed in this procedure. The bin edges are determined as follows: 1) the width of the first and the last edges' include most of the GeV and sub-GeV photons (far away points are excluded); 2) the inner edges are determined to be the n-equal diversion points in time of each adjacent lined GeV points; 3) the bin-edge division ratio (rate) is tested from 0.01 to 0.99 with an increment of 0.01 (for example, a rate equal to 0.2 means left five-division point). %Since the sample is small, a permutation method is used to calculate the p-value. When the rates range from 0.31 to 0.38, $\rho = 0.94$ with $p = 0.017$ as the blue line shows; $\rho = 0.83 $ with $p = 0.058$ when rates range from 0.75 to 0.96 as the green line shows; The red line represents $\rho = 0.71$ and $p = 0.136$ with rates ranging from 0.97 to 0.99. The orange line represents all other rates with a result of $\rho = 1.0$ with $p = 0.003$. 


As shown in Figure.~\ref{fig:observation} (c), different energy envelopes of sub-GeV photons extracted with different bin edges; $\rho$ is determined to be  $> 0.714$ according to the minimal result of all sets of bin edges, which implies a strong correlation between $\gamma^{\rm envelop}_{\rm sub-GeV}$ and $\gamma_{\rm 1-5GeV}$\footnote{The grading standards and the corresponding correlation degrees are available online:~\href{https://www.statstutor.ac.uk/resources/uploaded/spearmans.pdf}{Spearman's correlation}. }.

