\subsection{modeling with and without gap}\label{sec: modeling}
 The gap is statistically significant as analyzed in Section~\ref{sec: probstudy}. To further confirm this conclusion, modelings are performed with and without considering the gap in the model. However, the statistic is not large enough to extract the parameters of a specific physical model (e.g., an absorption line described by a gaussian function); moreover, the center energy and width of the gap vary with time, as shown in Fig~\ref{fig:observation} (c). Thus, the result of modeling with the gap subtracted in the bin is compared with that of all bins.
The Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) fitting is performed to find the parameters with maximum likelihood. The Sampling tool is $emcee$~\citep{2013PASP..125..306F}. \cite{2016MNRAS.463.1144W} suggested a Bayesian information criterion (BIC$=-2\rm{ln} \mathscr{L} +\rm{ln} (N)n$, $\mathscr{L}$ denotes the likelihood
function for all these parameters based on a Bayesian prior, $\rm N$ is the number of data points, and $\rm n$ is the number of free parameters) as a tool for model selection; a model that has a lower BIC value than the others is preferred. 
As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:maxEobsGeV_dTin} (b), the extracted indices of PL function are similar in the modelings with and without the gap; $\Delta$BIC=5.7, the preference for the gap in the PL model is positive~\footnote{If the change of BIC between these two models, $\Delta$BIC is from 2 to 6, the preference for the model with the lower BIC is positive; if $\Delta$BIC is from 6 to 10, the preference for that is strong; and if $\Delta$BIC is above 10, the preference is very strong.}. 




  

\section{The physical interpretation}\label{sec:physical_interpretation}
To account for the gap and correlation between $\gamma^{\rm envelop}_{\rm sub-GeV}$ and $\gamma_{\rm 1-5GeV}$, some physical interpretations could be proposed naturally: sub-GeV photons and GeV photons are not produced from the same mechanism (see the details in Section~\ref{sec: multi}); or the photons produced between $\gamma^{\rm envelop}_{\rm sub-GeV}$ and $\gamma_{\rm 1-5GeV}$, are absorbed by the resonant nuclide absorption scenario (see the details in Section~\ref{sec:absorption}). In order to distinguish between possible interpretations, an estimate of $\Gamma$ is performed in Section~\ref{sec:esti}.

\subsection{The estimate of $\Gamma$ with time and the allowed maximum energies of photons}\label{sec:esti}
 A model-dependent approach~\citep[e.g.,][]{2015ApJ...801..103G,2024ApJ...961..137S} is performed to obtain the estimate of the Lorentz Factor, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:maxEobsGeV_dTin}. For GRB 210704A, $\Gamma$ could be estimated using the `top-down' approach~\citep{2015ApJ...801..103G,2024ApJ...961..137S}. Here, a finer binning (bin size of 0.2 s) around the peak of the fluxes is used to determine the parameters of the mBB model. The fluxes and $\Gamma$ after $T_0$+0.5 s are estimated per 0.1 s with fixed parameters from the above modeling, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:maxEobsGeV_dTin} (b). \footnote{With the method described in ~\cite{2024ApJ...961..137S} and $Y=1$ ($Y$ is the ratio between the total fireball energy and the energy emitted), all bins are found to be in the regime of $r_{\rm ra}\leq r_{\rm ph}\leq r_{\rm c}$, where $r_{\rm ph}$ denotes the photosphere radius,  $r_{\rm ra}$ is the rapid acceleration radius, and $r_{\rm c}$ is the coasting radius. The estimated values of $\Gamma$ have uncertainties of about 10 to 20; their central values are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:maxEobsGeV_dTin} (b).}. $\Gamma$ reaches the maximum value ($\sim300$) around the flux peak of the MeV emissions and drops to $\sim 180$ around $T_0+1.2$ s. 
The corresponding radius are also estimated...
 
 Based on the analysis in Section~\ref{sec:obs.1}, after $T_0+1.5$ s, there may be a considerable contribution from non-thermal emission since $\alpha$ is around the synchrotron line. Due to the possible uncertainty regarding the changes in Y, $\Gamma$ $\Gamma$ can hardly be estimated and is not shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:maxEobsGeV_dTin} (b) after $T_0+1.5$s.
 % and after around $T_0+1.5$ s, there may be considerable contribution from non-thermal emission since $\alpha$ is around the synchrotron line. Due to the low statistics and the uncertainties on the changes of Y, 
