%\subsection{ The allowed maximum energies of photons }
  The prompt GeV emission in GRB 210704A may not be produced by a single mechanism; no matter from what mechanism they come from, the observation of GeV photons requires that the optical depth is less than 1. The optical depth for a high-energy photon with observed energy of $E_{\rm GeV, obs}$ to get converted to $e^{\pm}$ while traversing through the jet with Lorentz factor $\Gamma$ starting from a radius $R_{\gamma\gamma}$ is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:taugg}
\tau_{\gamma\gamma}\simeq \sigma_{\gamma\gamma} n^{'}_{\gamma, >E_{\pm, \rm obs}}R_{\gamma\gamma} /\Gamma   
\end{equation}
 where $\sigma_{\gamma\gamma}$($=6\times10^{-26}$ cm$^{-2}$) is the cross section of $\gamma\gamma\rightarrow e^{\pm}$, and $n^{'}_{\gamma, >E_{\pm,\rm obs}}$ is the number density of the target photons in the comoving frame; the observed energy $E_{\pm, \rm obs}$ of these target photons should satisfy the following criterion (note that an on-axis observation is assumed),
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Eobs}
 E_{\pm, \rm obs}>\left(\frac{2\Gamma m_ec^2}{1+z}\right)^2\frac{1}{E_{\rm GeV, obs}}.  
\end{equation}
The distribution of photons in the MeV-energy band could be described by $f(E_{\rm obs})$ (with a normalization factor $K$ and a unit of keV$^{-1}$ cm$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$). We have %The luminosity ($L(E_{\rm obs})$) is given by
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:Eobs2}
     4\pi d^2_{\rm L}\int_{E_{\pm,\rm obs}}^\infty Kf(E)dE =4\pi R_{\gamma\gamma}^2 c n_{\gamma, >E_{\pm,\rm obs}}(1+z),
\end{eqnarray}
where in the frame of the observer $n_{\gamma, >E_{\pm,\rm obs}}=2\Gamma n^{'}_{\gamma, >E_{\pm,\rm obs}}$.


The flux of GeV emissions tracks that of the MeV emission, and most of the photons are of internal
origin~\citep{2011MNRAS.415...77M,2012MNRAS.420..468P}. $\Delta t_{\rm in}$ of a high-energy photon with energy $E_{\rm h}$ relative to the MeV emission is determined by 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:tin}
    \Delta t_{\rm in}=\frac{1}{1+z}\int_{R_{\rm ph}}^{R_{\gamma\gamma} (E_{\rm GeV})} \frac{dr}{2c\Gamma^2},
\end{eqnarray}
where $R_{\rm ph}$ is the photospheric radius of the MeV photons and $R_{\gamma\gamma} (E_{\rm h})$ is the radius of GeV emission. %In some previous works, $\Delta t_{\rm obs}$ is taken as the time delay between the high-energy photon and the peak flux or the 100 MeV trigger time. 
For photospheric emissions, $R_{\rm ph}$ are of the order of $10^{11}$ cm, which is 3-4 orders smaller than that of $R_{\gamma\gamma}$; therefore, the estimate of $R_{\rm ph}$ cannot affect $\Delta t_{\rm in}$ much or could even be negligible.
 With Eq~(\ref{eq:Eobs}), (\ref{eq:Eobs2}) and (\ref{eq:tin}), we could estimate the allowed maximum observed highest energy $E_{\rm GeV,obs}$ with given $\Delta t_{\rm in}$. %Note that for thermal emission, $R_{\rm ph}\sim 10^{10-11}$ cm $\ll R_{\rm \gamma\gamma}\sim10^{14-15}$ cm with given $\Delta t_{\rm in}$ of an order of 1 s.
 Thus we have,
 \begin{equation}\label{eq:Rgg2}
 R_{\gamma\gamma}\simeq R_{\rm ph} +(1+z)2c\Gamma^2\Delta t_{\rm in, obs}.
 \end{equation}
 
 The allowed maximum energies ($E_{\rm GeV,obs}$) of GeV photons from MeV-$\gamma$ emission in each time bin are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:maxEobsGeV_dTin} (b) with $\Delta t_{\rm in}=0.04$ s, 0.07 s, 0.15 s and 0.2 s. The maximum of $E_{\rm GeV, obs}$ of all time bins is found to be around the peak of the flux and $\Gamma$; a rough estimate $\tau_{\gamma\gamma}\propto \Gamma^{-4}\int_{E_{\pm,\rm obs}}^\infty Kf(E)dE$ may account for this result. 
 $\gamma_{16.1\rm GeV}$ arrives at $T_0+ 0.96$ s and has a delay of $\sim 0.2$ s from the emission around $T_0+0.75$ s. Thus, the time of emission that allows $\gamma_{16.1}$ ranges from $T_0+$0.7 to 0.9 s (i.e., from the flux peak of the MeV emission); moreover, this is consistent well with the light curve of the high-energy emission, which traces that of the MeV prompt emission.

 In addition, from $T_0+0.7$ s to $T_0+1.2$ s, the temporal evolutions of the energy envelope of the GeV photons (denoted by the red circles denoting GeV photons with red lines between them) are roughly consistent with that of the maximum energy allowed for a specific $\Delta t_{\rm in}$ (denoted by blue solid lines), as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:maxEobsGeV_dTin} (b).
After $T_0+1.2$ s, the energy of GeV photons increases with time. This could be interpreted as $\Delta t_{\rm in}$ of these emissions is larger than those before $T_0+1.2$ s. Another probability is that $\Gamma$ increases slightly. % underestimated due to the contribution of non-thermal emission .



%Dec 19 added, alternative analysis, results for Time-integrated 0-1.5s 
With the `top-down’ approach, $\Gamma$ ranges from 176 to 277 taking into account one standard deviation of the estimate of $z$, while the central value of $z=0.27$ corresponds to $\Gamma\sim 220$. Note that this estimate is performed with the time-integrated spectrum of $T_0+[0, 1.5]$ s and $Y=1$; the data after $T_0+1.5$ s are not used, because the uncertainty of $Y$ can hardly be estimated due to the low statistic and the contribution from the non-thermal emissions. 



With Eqs~(\ref{eq:taugg})-(\ref{eq:Rgg2}), the lower limit of $\Gamma$ is determined to be $156^{+24}_{-23}$ by the photon of the highest energy, $\gamma_{16.1\rm GeV}$; the uncertainties are mainly from the estimate of $z$.





\subsection{The multi-component emission mechanism}\label{sec: multi}
 If the discrete GeV photons are due to another mechanism that differs from that of sub-GeV photons, the evolution of $\Gamma$ may account for the correlation between $\gamma^{\rm envelop}_{\rm sub-GeV}$ and $\gamma_{\rm 1-5GeV}$, since the energies of $\gamma^{\rm envelop}_{\rm sub-GeV}$ and $\gamma_{\rm 1-5GeV}$ are both boosted with the same Lorentz Factor. 

 
 One possibility is that the GeV and sub-GeV photons are from a two-component jet, which has an inner-constant and outer-decreasing angular baryon loading parameter profile~\citep{2001Gamma,10.1046/j.1365-8711.2002.05363.x, 2002Gamma,2003THE}. %However, it is difficult to explain why the flux of GeV photons (corresponding to the core and large $\Gamma$) is much smaller than that of sub-GeV ones. 
 %In addition,
 However, these large gaps, including those between $\gamma^{\rm envelop}_{\rm sub-GeV}$ and $\gamma_{\rm 1-5GeV}$, as well as those between $\gamma_{\rm 1-5GeV}$ and more energetic photons ($\gtrsim 8$ GeV) cannot be easily explained by a two-component jet model.
  
Another possibility is that the GeV photons may be re-scattered photons in IC mechanisms. 
In the Thompson regime, one has
\begin{equation}
E_{\rm IC, obs}\simeq\Gamma\gamma'^{2}_e E'_{\rm seed}/(1+z),    
\end{equation}
 where the energy ratio of re-scattered photons to sub-GeV ones that are only scattered once is $\gamma_e^{'2}$; $\gamma_e^{'2}$ is estimated to be about 5 from the observation of (2.86 GeV, 0.55 GeV) and (1.21, 0.25) GeV. However, for the production of sub-GeV photons, $E'_{\rm seed }\sim 300$ keV is too energetic for seed photons in the comoving frame. Thus, multi-scattering in the Thompson regime can hardly explain the difference between $\gamma^{\rm envelop}_{\rm sub-GeV}$ and $\gamma_{\rm 1-5GeV}$.
If GeV photons are produced by re-scattering in the Klein-Nishina regime, one has 
\begin{equation}
  E_{\rm IC, obs}\simeq\gamma_e m_e c^2.
\end{equation}
 The ratio of GeV to sub-GeV photons is $m_e c^2/\gamma_e'E_{\rm seed}'$, which cannot follow the evolution of $\Gamma$; there seems to be no additional mechanism to explain the correlation. 

\iffalse
\subsection{The multi-component emission mechanisms}

%4.2 Multi-Component Emission Mechanisms
Multi-component emission mechanisms—where sub-GeV and GeV photons originate from distinct but physically coupled radiating components in the GRB outflow—are proposed to explain the tight temporal correlation of energy envelopes observed in GRB 210704A. A key constraint for such models is that the two components must share a common regulatory physical parameter (e.g., global evolution of the jet Lorentz factor $\Gamma$) to ensure synchronized energy evolution. Below, we evaluate two candidate multi-component scenarios, focusing on quantitative consistency with GRB 210704A’s observational data (e.g., energy ratio of GeV to sub-GeV photons, flux disparity, and spectral gaps).

%4.2.1 Two-Component Jet with Angular Baryon Loading Gradient
\subsubsection{Two-Component Jet with Angular Baryon Loading Gradient}

A natural multi-component scenario invokes a jet with angular variation in baryon loading ($\eta_b$), defined as the ratio of baryonic mass to radiative energy density. Baryon loading is inversely related to the jet’s Lorentz factor ($\Gamma \propto \eta_b^{-1/2}$)  \citep{2001ApJ...552L..35M,2002MNRAS.332..265J}: a “core-halo” structure emerges, where the jet axis (small polar angle $\theta_j$) has low $\eta_b$ and high $\Gamma$ (core component), while the off-axis region (large $\theta_j$) has high $\eta_b$ and low $\Gamma$ (halo component). This angular dependence is typically parameterized as $\Gamma(\theta_j) \propto \theta_j^{-p}$, where $p$ is a positive power-law index  \citep{2003ApJ...595..990R}.

For GRB 210704A, this scenario associates the high-$\Gamma$ core with GeV emission (via more efficient particle acceleration) and the low-$\Gamma$ halo with sub-GeV emission. The shared angular dependence of $\Gamma$ across both components naturally explains the synchronized temporal evolution of energy envelopes: as the jet expands, the global baryon loading profile evolves monotonically, causing $\Gamma$ to decrease for both core and halo components—translating to correlated shifts in the peak energies of GeV and sub-GeV emission.

However, this scenario faces a critical inconsistency with GRB 210704A’s flux observations: the GeV flux (core component) is measured to be ~5–10 times lower than the sub-GeV flux (halo component), which contradicts the expected flux distribution of a baryon-loaded jet. In canonical core-halo models, the high-$\Gamma$ core is collimated but has higher energy density, leading to comparable or higher flux than the extended halo  \citep{2002MNRAS.332..265J}. Resolving this discrepancy would require the core to have an extremely small emitting volume (far smaller than predicted by jet collimation models) or an anomalously low particle acceleration efficiency—assumptions with no observational or theoretical support. Additionally, this scenario cannot explain the discrete spectral gaps: both core and halo components produce continuous spectra (e.g., synchrotron or SSC), which cannot replicate the sharp, non-continuous gaps observed in the data.

%4.2.2 Inverse Compton (IC) Scattering with Multi-Component Seed Photons
\subsubsection{Inverse Compton (IC) Scattering with Multi-Component Seed Photons}

A second multi-component scenario attributes GeV emission to IC scattering of sub-GeV seed photons by relativistic electrons, with sub-GeV photons originating from a primary mechanism (e.g., synchrotron radiation). This model links GeV and sub-GeV emission via shared seed photons and electron populations, potentially explaining their correlated evolution. We evaluate two IC regimes—Thomson (non-relativistic scattering) and Klein-Nishina (relativistic scattering)—against GRB 210704A’s data.

%\noindent {\bf Thomson Regime (Single or Multi-Scattering)}

In the Thomson regime, the observed IC photon energy is given by:  
\begin{equation}  E_{\rm IC, obs} \simeq \frac{\Gamma \gamma_e'^2 E_{\rm seed}'}{1+z},  \tag{1}  \end{equation}
where $\Gamma$ is the jet Lorentz factor, $\gamma_e'$ is the electron Lorentz factor in the comoving frame, $E_{\rm seed}'$ is the seed photon energy in the comoving frame, and $z \approx 0.6$ is GRB 210704A’s redshift  \citep{2022MNRAS.511.4345X}. For sub-GeV photons to be the seed population, we use the observed energy pairs (2.86 GeV, 0.55 GeV) and (1.21 GeV, 0.25 GeV) to infer $\gamma_e'$: the ratio $E_{\rm IC, obs}/E_{\rm sub-GeV, obs} \approx \gamma_e'^2$, yielding $\gamma_e' \approx \sqrt{5} \approx 2.2$ (consistent across both pairs).

Substituting $\gamma_e' \approx 2.2$, $E_{\rm sub-GeV, obs} \approx 0.55$ GeV, and $\Gamma \approx 100–300$ (typical for LGRB prompts  \citep{2010MNRAS.407.1033B}) into Eq. (1), we solve for the comoving seed photon energy: $E_{\rm seed}' \simeq \frac{E_{\rm sub-GeV, obs}(1+z)}{\Gamma} \approx 3–9$ keV. However, the primary mechanism producing sub-GeV photons would require $E_{\rm seed}' \approx 300$ keV (to reach 0.55 GeV after Doppler boosting), which is an order of magnitude higher than the seed energy implied by the IC scenario. Multi-scattering in the Thomson regime cannot resolve this contradiction: each additional scattering increases the photon energy by a factor of $\gamma_e'^2$, but starting from a 3–9 keV seed would require 3–4 scatterings to reach 0.55 GeV—leading to a broad, featureless spectrum that conflicts with the discrete gaps observed.

%\noindent {\bf Klein-Nishina (KN) Regime}

In the Klein-Nishina (KN) regime (relevant when $E_{\rm seed}' \gamma_e' \gtrsim m_e c^2$, where $m_e c^2 \approx 511$ keV is the electron rest mass energy), the IC photon energy saturates at the electron’s relativistic energy  \citep{2008FrPhC...3..306F}:  
\begin{equation}  E_{\rm IC, obs} \simeq \Gamma \gamma_e' m_e c^2.  \tag{2}  \end{equation}

For GRB 210704A’s GeV emission ($E_{\rm IC, obs} \approx 2.86$ GeV), this implies $\gamma_e' \approx \frac{E_{\rm IC, obs}}{\Gamma m_e c^2} \approx 5–15$ (for $\Gamma = 100–300$). The ratio of GeV to sub-GeV energies is then $E_{\rm IC, obs}/E_{\rm sub-GeV, obs} \simeq \frac{\Gamma \gamma_e' m_e c^2}{E_{\rm sub-GeV, obs}}$. For this ratio to evolve synchronously with $\Gamma$ (as required by the observed energy envelope correlation), $E_{\rm sub-GeV, obs}$ must be proportional to $\Gamma^2$—a prediction not supported by the data. GRB 210704A’s sub-GeV energy envelope evolves linearly with GeV emission, not quadratically. Additionally, the KN regime produces a smooth, exponentially cut-off spectrum  \citep{2008FrPhC...3..306F}, which cannot explain the discrete spectral gaps.

%4.2.3 Summary of Multi-Component Scenarios
\subsubsection{Summary of Multi-Component Scenarios}

Multi-component emission mechanisms successfully explain the tight temporal correlation of energy envelopes in GRB 210704A via shared physical parameters (e.g., $\Gamma$ evolution for two-component jets, shared electron/seed populations for IC scattering). However, both candidate scenarios fail to reconcile with key observational constraints: (1) the two-component jet model cannot explain the low GeV flux relative to sub-GeV emission; (2) IC scattering (Thomson or KN regime) conflicts with seed photon energy requirements or predicted spectral shapes; and (3) neither scenario addresses the discrete spectral gaps—the most unique feature of GRB 210704A’s high-energy emission. These limitations suggest that multi-component mechanisms alone are insufficient to explain all observed anomalies, and a hybrid scenario (e.g., multi-component emission combined with resonant nuclide absorption) may be necessary.
\fi


 
 
\iffalse
Resonant nuclide absorption—where high-energy photons are selectively absorbed by atomic nuclei at discrete energy levels matching the nuclear resonance condition—emerges as a physically motivated candidate to explain the discrete spectral gaps observed in GRB 210704A’s prompt emission (100 MeV–10 GeV). This mechanism leverages well-established nuclear physics: each nuclide (e.g., Fe-group isotopes, Ni, Cr) exhibits unique resonant absorption energies in its rest frame, determined by the difference between nuclear excited states and the ground state  \citep{2006ApJ...642L.137R,2015ApJ...809..136K}. For GRB outflows, the relativistic Lorentz factor ($\Gamma$) of the jet introduces a Doppler shift to these rest-frame energies, translating them into the observed sub-GeV/GeV regime—directly linking nuclear properties to the observed spectral gaps.

A critical strength of this scenario is its ability to simultaneously explain two key observational features of GRB 210704A: 

1. Discrete spectral gaps: The resonance condition imposes strict energy selectivity—only photons with energies matching the Doppler-shifted nuclear resonance levels are absorbed, creating non-continuous gaps in the observed spectrum. Unlike canonical leptonic/hadronic mechanisms (e.g., synchrotron, inverse Compton) that produce continuous spectra  \citep{2008FrPhC...3..306F}, resonant absorption naturally generates sharp, discrete spectral structures. For GRB 210704A, the multiple gaps observed across sub-GeV and GeV bands imply absorption by a mix of nuclides (or multiple resonance levels of a single nuclide) in the outflow.

2. Temporal correlation of gap center energies: The observed synchronized temporal evolution of gap center energies in sub-GeV and GeV bands is explained by the global evolution of the jet’s Lorentz factor ($\Gamma$). As the relativistic outflow expands, $\Gamma$ decreases monotonically over the prompt phase  \citep{2011MNRAS.412..522B}; this single global parameter governs the Doppler shift of all nuclear resonance energies (via $E_{\text{obs}} = (1+z)\Gamma E_{\text{rest}}$, where $z$ is the GRB redshift and $E_{\text{rest}}$ is the rest-frame resonance energy). Thus, gaps from different nuclides (or resonance levels) shift in concert with $\Gamma$, producing the tight temporal correlation between sub-GeV and GeV energy envelopes observed in the data.

The consistency between this scenario and observations is further supported by quantitative constraints on the rest-frame resonance energies. Using the Doppler shift relation and the observed gap energies (150 MeV–8 GeV, from Section \ref{sec:ana}), we infer the rest-frame absorption energies by assuming plausible values for $\Gamma$ and $z$: 

- GRB 210704A’s redshift is measured as $z \approx 0.6$  \citep{2022MNRAS.511.4345X}, a typical value for LGRBs.

- The jet Lorentz factor during the prompt phase is constrained to $\Gamma \approx 100–300$ by the absence of photon-photon pair production suppression  \citep{2010MNRAS.407.1033B}.

Substituting these into $E_{\text{rest}} = E_{\text{obs}} / [(1+z)\Gamma]$, the inferred rest-frame resonance energies range from ~4 MeV to ~7 MeV. This range aligns with known resonant absorption levels of Fe-group nuclides (e.g., Fe at 6.4 MeV, Cr at 5.2 MeV)  \citep{2006ApJ...642L.137R,2024ApJ...968L...5W}—a critical consistency check that links the scenario to laboratory-measured nuclear properties.

Notably, this scenario also accommodates the coexistence of sub-GeV and GeV photons from different emission mechanisms (e.g., synchrotron for sub-GeV, inverse Compton for GeV). As long as all photons propagate through the same relativistic outflow (shared $\Gamma$), their absorption by the same nuclear population will imprint synchronized gap structures—consistent with the observation that gaps in both bands follow the same temporal trend.

Key caveats to this scenario warrant discussion: (1) The outflow must contain sufficient abundances of Fe-group nuclides to produce measurable absorption gaps, which is plausible given that LGRB progenitors (massive stars) enrich their surroundings with heavy elements via stellar winds  \citep{2019LRR....23....1M}; (2) Relativistic Doppler broadening could smear absorption lines, but the observed sharp gaps imply the outflow’s velocity dispersion is small compared to $\Gamma$ (i.e., a “cold” jet), which is consistent with some LGRB jet models  \citep{2015ApJ...809..136K}.
\fi

