%Multi-component emission mechanisms represent a plausible approach to explain the tight temporal correlation of sub-GeV and GeV energy envelopes in GRB 210704A, relying on physically coupled radiating components sharing a common regulatory parameter (e.g., the jet's Lorentz factor $\Gamma$) \citep{2002MNRAS.332..265J,2008FrPhC...3..306F}. One candidate variant invokes a two-component jet with angular baryon loading gradients, where a high-$\Gamma$ core (linked to GeV emission) and low-$\Gamma$ halo (sub-GeV emission) exhibit synchronized energy evolution via shared $\Gamma$ decay. Another possibility involves inverse Compton (IC) scattering, where GeV photons arise from upscattered sub-GeV seed photons, with the two bands correlated through common relativistic electron populations and seed photon fields. While these mechanisms offer a coherent framework for the observed temporal correlation, their ability to fully reconcile with other key features (e.g., discrete spectral gaps) requires further observational and theoretical validation.

\subsection{The Resonant Nuclide Absorption Scenario and Other Potential Absorption Mechanism}
\label{sec:resonant-nuclide-absorption}\label{sec:absorption}

Applying the Doppler correction $E_{\rm rest} \approx E_{\rm obs} (1+z) / 2\Gamma$, where $z \simeq 0.27$ is the redshift derived from the Amati relation and jet modeling \cite{2023MNRAS.522.5204B}, the observed gap of $0.6-1.2$ GeV translates to a comoving energy range of:
\begin{equation}
E_{\rm gap}^{\prime} \approx 2 - 5 \text{ MeV},
\end{equation}
where the lower (upper) limit corresponds to 0.62 (1.21) GeV and $\Gamma\simeq 220$; Taking into account the robust and model-independent lower limit of $\Gamma\sim 160$ determined by $\gamma_{16.1 \rm GeV}$ and the maximum upper limit of the gap (2.86 GeV), $E_{\rm gap}^{\prime}$ cannot exceed 10 MeV. 

